  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=-9+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $1$, find the equation.",
  "worked_solution": "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=-9+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=-9-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-(-9+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}))(x-(-9-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}))=0\n$$\n\nBy expanding the parentheses, we can write the equation in standard form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x(-9+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i})-x(-9-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i})+(-9+\\sqrt{2}\\text{i})(-9-\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+9x-x\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}+9x+x\\sqrt{2}\\text{i}+81-2{\\text{i}}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+18x+81+2 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+18x+83 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$",
